Date: 2026-11-04 · Status: Proposed. Ahmad's message of 2026-11-04 asked to continue on `claude/replica-design-jarvis` and to:
1. make the contrast gate read Jarvis tokens;
2. finalise the type and spacing measurements and vendor offline Latin and Arabic fonts;
3. add Apple Liquid Glass within DESIGN.md;
4. add a live Studio and a Skill Mixer;
5. add `replica-architect` and `replica-build` (plus `replica-test` and `replica-diff`) after a security review;
6. run `/replica-architect` and `/replica-build`, then open a PR.
The merge waits for his go.
ADR-132 left Jarvis with tokens and a `/design` page, but three things open:
- `audits/verify-contrast.mjs` could not read Jarvis role names and crashed on them.
- `measurements.md` said "estimated".
- Inter was not vendored.
Ahmad also asked for "Fable 5 standard measurements". No published design standard by that name could be found (web search on 2026-11-04 returned only unrelated brands named Fable). So the measurements are recorded as final decisions with real sources (the font files, the contrast gate, DESIGN.md), and none of them is attributed to "Fable 5".
1. **Contrast gate.** `audits/verify-contrast.mjs` detects a file whose `:root` uses Jarvis role names and checks Jarvis pairs instead:
   - every text colour on bg, surface, surface-raised and surface-sunken;
   - the three fills;
   - text on glass, composited in sRGB over bg, surface and surface-raised.
   It checks the default theme (from `color-scheme`), the other theme, and P3 variants if present. On every OMEGA file its output is byte-identical to before.
2. **Fonts, all OFL 1.1, from Fontsource 5.3.0** (published 2026-07-19; fetched with `npm pack`, no scripts run). They live in `jarvis/fonts/` with their licences:
   - Inter Variable (Latin UI);
   - Geist Mono Variable (numbers, code);
   - Readex Pro Variable (Arabic), chosen over IBM Plex Sans Arabic because it is one variable file.
   `jarvis/scripts/font-metrics.mjs` reads their metrics from the WOFF

Date: 2026-11-13 · Status: Proposed.
Ahmad's message of 2026-11-13 asked for `#/polymath`, which puts one question to all of the repository's engines through five stages:
1. cross-domain decomposition after the privacy shield (ADR-158);
2. Munger inversion;
3. a weighted decision matrix from the person's own weights;
4. provenance with explicit abstention, following VeritasGraph;
5. an RFC 6962 Merkle root with a 64-character hash that wraps at 375 px.
He also asked for:
- the same answers in Node and Python;
- a read-only review of VeritasGraph, Semantica, the Munger / WDM method and K-Dense's skills;
- the stale parity line in `jarvis/README.md` fixed.
He named the Python file `.claude/skills/replica-polymath-engine/`, by this repository's convention.
1. **The review** is `docs/security/POLYMATH-SOURCES-REVIEW-2026-11-13.md`.
   - **What was read:** three MIT projects, one list without a licence file, and K-Dense, which ADR-159 already reviewed. Nothing was installed, and SkillSpector found 0 / 0 / 0 / 0 in the downloaded text.
   - **What was taken:** only the stated rules — `grounded`, `unknown_citations` and `abstained`; decisions as records with their source; WDM with Munger inversion.
2. **The engine** is `jarvis/server/lib/polymath-engine.mjs` (pure, inlined into the page in its own scope over the existing scopes). Its twin is `.claude/skills/replica-polymath-engine/polymath_engine.py` (standard library, over the reviewed skills). Both run five stages:
   1. **Decompose.**
      - **Cleaning:** the privacy shield cleans the question.
      - **Naming a domain:** each of the five domains (maths, time series, optics, design, security) is named in one of three ways: the person picks it in `domains`; one of its fixed words is in the question [INFERENCE]; or its input is given.
      - **Running:** a named domain runs its engine when its input is there:
        - maths: `solve` (ADR-154);
        - time series: `analyse` (ADR-147), 2 to 5,000 points;
        - op

Date: 2026-11-13 · Status: Proposed. Ahmad's message of 2026-11-13: «صمم تصميم الشجره و جذوره و ركب المهارات و ثبت الادوات و المهارات و خليهم يعملون بطريقه احترافيه» — design the tree and its roots, compose the skills, install the tools and skills, and make them work together.
1. **What "install" means here.** The 57 skills in `.claude/skills` are already installed: Claude Code loads them from that folder, and every Jarvis engine they back is wired into the server and the page.
   - The tree shows each one's real state. It installs nothing new.
   - Outside skills and tools still need a written review and Ahmad's approval (AGENTS.md §1), so none were added.
   - The new skill is `replica-skill-tree`, written here.
2. **The snapshot.** `.claude/skills/replica-skill-tree/skill_tree.py snapshot` reads only the repository's files and writes `jarvis/app/data/skill-tree-input.json`. Per skill, it records:
   - its tools and function counts, from `knowledge-graph.json`;
   - its SkillSpector findings by severity, from `skills-scan.json`;
   - whether it has `tests/` and a golden file;
   - its mutation result (killed out of non-equivalent mutants), from `mutation-audit.log`;
   - the first security review that names it.
3. **The engine.** `jarvis/server/lib/skill-tree.mjs` is pure and has no dependencies. It is inlined into the page in its own scope, and `skill_tree.py` gives the same answers (`tests/golden.json`, 43 requests).
   - **Roots**, drawn below a ground line:
     - AGENTS.md and DESIGN.md;
     - the approval locks;
     - the state prover (ADR-145);
     - the Merkle log (ADR-147);
     - the privacy shield (ADR-158);
     - the contrast healer (ADR-153).
   - **Trunk:** the workbench (ADR-160), the polymath engine (ADR-161) and the universal agent (ADR-159). Each lists the skills it already runs together.
   - **Branches:** Jarvis skills (`replica-*`), OMEGA skills (the project skills CLAUDE.md lists), and vendored references.
   - **Leaves:** each skill's to

Date: 2026-11-15 · Status: Proposed. Ahmad's message of 2026-11-15 asked to finish the name change inside the pages and the workspace title, keeping folders, `jarvis/server/server.mjs` and code paths as they are, and to show a corrected plan in `#/runbook` when a stage is refused for an input the repository can correct by rule.
1. **The repaired plan.** `runbook.mjs` and `runbook.py` gain `repairRequest` / `repair_request`. When a run is not ready, each refused stage is checked for an input the existing rules can correct:
   - **order:** skills that are not in `skill-deps.json` are left out, repeated names are joined, and at most 12 are kept;
   - **plan:** a `c` that UCB1 refuses is left out, and each record entry that the plan rule refuses on its own is left out (a record that is not an object is left out whole);
   - **tree:** a CSS that the design stage cannot read is left out.
   Each change names its stage, its field and what was left out. The repaired request is run through the six stages once more, and the answer adds `repair: {changes, request, request_sha256, after: {ready, locked, stages, chain_sha256}}`. With nothing to correct, `repair` is null. The repaired request never includes the goal, the secret or the CSS.
2. **The CSS, corrected.** The runbook takes an optional `css`. It goes to the workbench's design stage. The tree stage also shows:
   - the reversal engine's and contrast healer's rules (ADR-150, ADR-153);
   - the SHA-256 before and after;
   - the corrected CSS;
   - `fixed_point`: whether the corrected copy needs no further change when run again.
   A gradient is not a refusal here, because those engines already correct it. The tree stage is refused only when the workbench tree fails, which a CSS it cannot read causes.
3. **Locks.** Nothing in the repair sends, installs or approves. Locked steps stay locked in both runs, and `locked` counts them.
4. **The name in the text.** Every interface sentence that named Jarvis now says «أثير برايم» i

Date: 2026-11-15 · Status: Proposed. Ahmad's message of 2026-11-15 asked to research the strongest AI models and the best free open-weight ones, and to develop the site. This ADR covers the part that can be settled from facts: the model catalog that Settings and the local engines already use (ADR-140, ADR-143).
- `jarvis/server/lib/model-catalog.mjs` held 12 families with licences "as known on 2026-11-06" and no named source per row.
- Since then several makers have published new open weights:
  - DeepSeek V4;
  - Qwen3.8;
  - GLM-5.2 and 5.3;
  - Kimi K3 and K3.1 Pro;
  - Gemma 4, which moved to Apache 2.0;
  - Mistral Large 4;
  - Devstral Small 2;
  - MiMo-V2.6;
  - Olmo 3;
  - Granite Switch 4.1.
- Web articles that rank these models disagree on sizes and repeat makers' benchmark claims. AGENTS.md §3 asks for a named source and a date for every figure.
1. **One source per row.** Each family names the Hugging Face model record it was read from (`card`), read on 2026-11-15 through `https://huggingface.co/api/models/<id>`. The fields are:
   - `licence`: the record's `license` field;
   - `created`: the record's `createdAt` date;
   - `params`: the parameter count its safetensors files declare, or `null` when the record gives none (Mistral Large 4, whose card's own figures are kept in the note);
   - `read`: the day it was read.
2. **Custom licences.** A licence the record marks `other` is named by the title line of the repository's `LICENSE` file. That file was read only up to its conditions, so the conditions are not summarised; read the file before any commercial use. Mistral's licence is the exception: its Model-as-a-Service condition was read in full and is stated.
3. **30 families.**
   - The 12 earlier families were re-read; their licences matched.
   - 18 were added: deepseek-v4-pro, deepseek-v4-flash, qwen3.8-max, qwen3.8-flash, qwen3.8-27b, glm-5.3-flash, glm-5.3, glm-5.2, kimi-k3.1-pro, kimi-k3, mimo-v2.6-pro, mimo-v2.6-flash, gpt-oss, gemma-4, olmo-3, g
